7592186534936 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534936 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534936 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534936 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534936 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534936 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5d207ef-09f8-5f25-8bd3-232e40b6944c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534961 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534961 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534961 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534961 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f16ea21d-f232-5a8c-ae03-fff1dd1ddc4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534975 #edb/ref 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(-1,-4)$ and has velocity $⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ad91671-88c2-5e5d-8e1f-e5e0e6fbe489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534975 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},{e}^{-t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨4({0}^{3})+{C}_{1},{e}^{-0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-t}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,-4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-0}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-4⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=-3.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{4}-1,-{e}^{-t}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535013 #edb/ref 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(-10,-8)$ and has velocity $⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "581fcb98-ffbb-541a-98a8-b1aad8f2210c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535013 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+{C}_{1},\\,2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)+{C}_{1},\\,2(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=-6.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-6,\\,2t-6⟩.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-6,\\,2t-6⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨-10,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨(3)^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{3},\\,(3)^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-10,-8⟩ & =⟨-9+{C}_{3},\\,-9+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=1.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-6t-1,\\,{t}^{2}-6t+1⟩.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "990e6c9c-e46f-5a33-8086-74712d90c327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535044 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535073 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535073 #edb/ref 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-2,2t⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-4,-8⟩$ when $t=1,$ find the times when the particle is at the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9799b8b-4af9-5ece-b36d-864fdc8c4517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535073 #edb/ref 1074 "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-2,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-4,\\,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,(1)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,\\,-8⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=-9.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-2t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-2t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-9⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t-3=(t-3)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,3, \\\\ {t}^{2}-9=(t+3)(t-3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6t,\\,4t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-12,\\,-8⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the origin. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle is at the origin when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e49b201b-78f0-5668-9145-a0f21b5a41c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535076 #edb/ref 1074 "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 6t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-12,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-12,-8⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-12$ and ${C}_{2}=-8.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-12,\\,2{t}^{2}-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨0,0⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}-12,\\,2{t}^{2}-8⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12 & =3(t-2)(t+2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 2, \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-8 & =2(t-2)(t+2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle is at the origin when $t=-2$ and $t=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535097 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535097 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "197c6117-5c00-5077-8365-1a9c9af6dfe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535097 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2699fe8b-82db-50e1-8352-adb382bcf01e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "964e993d-11f9-583e-a087-b27639c1cb55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/r